## 📌 How to use this module

Read each topic in order and pay attention to the examples.

You do not need to memorize every detail on your first reading. Focus on understanding the role of each token and how the pieces work together.

> 💡 Think of this module as learning the alphabet and vocabulary of C. Once you know the building blocks, writing complete programs becomes easier.

## ❓ Common beginner doubts

### 1. Why do we need to learn tokens?
Because the compiler reads code as small meaningful parts. If you understand the basic building blocks, debugging becomes much easier.

### 2. Are spaces important?
Spaces mainly help humans read the code. The compiler usually ignores extra spaces between tokens.

### 3. What if I write a keyword as a variable name?
The compiler will reject it because keywords are reserved.

### 4. What is the difference between a constant and a string?
A constant is a fixed value like `25` or `3.14`. A string is text enclosed in double quotes like `"Hello"`.

### 5. What happens if I forget a semicolon?
The compiler usually reports a syntax error, because it expects the statement to finish.

## 🧪 Module practice

After completing all eight topics, try to identify every token in this program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int age = 20;
    printf("Age: %d\n", age);
    return 0;
}
```

Ask yourself:

- Which tokens are keywords?
- Which tokens are identifiers?
- Which tokens are constants?
- Which token is a string literal?
- Which tokens are operators or special symbols?

> 🌱 You do not need to solve this perfectly on the first attempt. The goal is to practice seeing a C program as a collection of meaningful building blocks.

